this predator should have brought the  plasma cannon and does that mask more  style than substance  the particular predator who arrives on  earth in prey has a ship that's shown a  handful of times mostly while it's  transitioning in and out of active  camouflage it's even visible as early as  the first scene where a slight  disturbance in the sky over naaru's  village foreshadows the alien's arrival  but what happens to the ship after the  predator descends to the ground in the  shots where the predator is properly  deployed he simply drops from his ship  which re-enters camouflage and  disappears into a nearby massive clouds  is the ship on autopilot are there other  hunters on board setting off for other  spots on earth or even other planets  what happens to it after naaru kills the  predator while these questions might all  seem minor in the grand scope of the  story their answers would provide some  interesting insight it's unknown just  how extensively the predators have  explored earth and humanity by 1719 and  getting a look inside the ship would  give some clues perhaps for the best  though prey is more interested in the  action on the ground leaving the many  mysteries of the ship unanswered  naru successfully uses the predator's  helmet-mounted lokon system to kill it  but when she returns to her tribe all  she carries of the creature is its  severed head what about the deployable  shield the active camouflage the armor  the weapons and all the other pieces of  advanced technology was it damaged  beyond use in the fight is it possible  for humans of the early 18th century to  decipher or does naru simply believe  that she and her people are better off  without such strange tools that last  option might be the most likely one frey  makes a point of showing how advanced  technology can be an untrustworthy ally  such as when the flintlock pistol given  to naru fails to fire and when the  predator is killed by its own weapon  ultimately instincts intelligence and  survival skills are shown to be far  superior to any piece of sci-fi tech  which might be why naru lets the  predator's gear rot in the mud or maybe  with the influx of european invaders  should simply rather leave the tools out  of potentially dangerous and treacherous  hands  at the end of predator 2 mike harrigan  is given a gift by an elder predator a  flintlock pistol from the year 1715 as a  sign of respect according to the name  engraved on the gun it once belonged to  rafael adelini while this moment is  mainly meant to emphasize just how long  the predators have hunted on earth the  pistol has become a key piece of  predator law at the end of prey it's  revealed that the flintlock pistol given  to naaru midway through the movie is the  same one seen in predator 2. the  character who gives her the gun is  credited in the film as rafael which  would seem to tie the whole thing up  nicely or it would if not for a few  lingering questions the big mystery  about the pistol now is how it got back  into the hands of the alien hunters  unless they specifically tracked it  which seems unlikely it would be  incredibly unlikely for them to  encounter whoever it got passed down to  decades or centuries later the most  logical explanation might be a pretty  dark one the predators return for naaru  later on defeat her and take the gun as  a trophy whatever the explanation the  return of the flintlock pistol is a  pretty notable retcon of previously  established predator law  depending on what predator stories  you're reading or watching you might  have one of several different  understandings of the history on earth  the alien vs predator movies show a  timeline where the species lived among  humans thousands of years in the past  and the comics show predators hunting  humans as early as 1718.  that might sound like it would line up  perfectly with the events of prey but  the fifth film in the franchise actually  retcons some of the previous lore pretty  severely in terms of how long the  hunters have been around the comic book  predator 1718 tells the story of rafael  adelini a pirate captain who encounters  a predator shortly before his death in  the comic he gives his engraved  flintlock pistol to one of the aliens as  a sign of respect it's the same gun  that's given back to humanity at the end  of predator 2.  but in prey that flintlock pistol is  given to naaru by a member of the french  trappers that would seem to undo the  entire 1718 story from any kind of canon  which means that the actual history of  predators on earth is once again pretty  murky at the beginning of the movie  naaru delivers a pretty interesting bit  of narration explaining that long ago a  monster had come to her land is she  speaking about a time before the events  of prey or after perhaps predators have  come to her home before but it's never  confirmed  there are some pretty big differences  between the predator and prey and the  ones seen in the other films and the  franchise primarily though the alien  hunter still has a lot of impressive  technology it's not nearly to the level  seen in later human hunts this is most  visible in one particularly glaring  absence a shoulder-mounted plasma cannon  that's typically been the predator's  most iconic and dangerous weapon in  prior films a plasma cannon is connected  to the triangular targeting laser used  by the predators to hunt down their  targets in prey however that targeting  apparatus is connected to a handheld  spear gun still a deadly weapon as is  proven multiple times but not quite as  advanced as the plasma cannon the  simplest explanation for the classic  weapons absence is that predator  technology just isn't quite as advanced  in prey which would make sense given  that it's several hundred years removed  from the rest of the series the predator  also has a mechanical shield rather than  an energy based one and a much simpler  helmet and suit of armor than those worn  by later hunters even still it seems odd  that a race that's achieved invisibility  and interstellar travel hasn't yet  mastered the science of big deadly laser  guns another possible explanation is  that the hunter in prey just chooses to  use simpler tools to make the hunt more  challenging  historically predators wear full helmets  to hide their mandibular alien faces in  prey however the alien hunter that  fights naaru sports a very different  piece of headgear more of a mask than a  helmet it looks like it could be part of  a skull possibly a trophy from a past  hunt modified with a laser tracking  system why the predator chooses to wear  the mask however remains unknown  ugly  at the very least the mask suggests that  this isn't the predator's first rodeo  the hunter has killed before but not  necessarily on earth with a spaceship at  the ready the mask could belong to any  number of as yet unnamed species across  the galaxy it's also unclear if using  this kind of trophy mask instead of the  more traditional helmet is the norm of  the time or a personal choice we don't  get to see another predator in prey but  the alien choosing the mask instead of  the classic armored helmet may be some  kind of prideful display  [Music]

